  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 137 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nToday - Wednesday: Cool and Dry\n\nA shortwave trough continues to make its way through the region. \nThis caused some scattered flurries across northern into central \nWisconsin this morning. Outside of these flurries, dry conditions \ncontinue through midweek. Looking at tonight's forecast, a surface \nhigh moves overhead and brings with it some of the coolest \ntemperatures of the season so far. Previous forecasts were a little \ncolder with temperatures, however with mid to high-level clouds \nentering the CWA during the early morning hours temperatures have \nslightly increased. Current forecasted lows are mostly in the mid \nteens. If the cloud cover is a little later in arriving then, with \nthe help of very light winds, these temperatures could drop a few \nmore degrees with some locations dropping into the upper single \ndigits particularly in the usual cold areas in Monroe and Jackson \ncounties. With the surface high still over the region on Tuesday and \nWednesday, high temperatures stay in the 30s for Tuesday and the mid \n30s to low 40s for Wednesday. \n\nThanksgiving: Precipitation Potential\n\nHeading into Thanksgiving we will be watching a complex setup \ninvolving two separate shortwaves. The first wave comes down into \nthe central plains on Wednesday with the second wave coming up from \nthe southern plains. These waves combine into one big upper level \nlow on Thanksgiving Day near the mid-Mississippi River Valley then \nshoot off to the northeast. As this low deepens, the warm sector \nwill pull moisture north and have it wrap around the low where the \ncoldest air is located. The corresponding warm front that pushes \nnorth actually stalls since the forward progression on the upper low \nis slowed with the wave interaction taking place. This warm front \nends up stalling in a north to south axis. Wherever this front ends \nup, will be where a prolonged period of snow could occur. \n\nTrends with this system have been a little back and forth over the \nlast couple of days. Yesterday's ML and AI guidance were \nhighlighting the potential for snow to make it up north into \nnortheast Iowa and southwest Wisconsin with a 10 to 30% chance for \nat least 1\" of snow to occur based on the 00Z LREF. The 06Z and 12Z \nguidance that has come in has shifted this further south to where \nthere is only a 10 to 20% chance for 1\" or more to occur for \nportions of northeast Iowa and far southwest Wisconsin. The trends \nare for the storm to impact some part of Illinois more than likely. \nThis doesn't mean our CWA is completely out of the woods though. \nGiven that this a complex interaction of two different waves, our \nCWA is a little too close for comfort to completely rule out any \nimpacts. This is shown by some outlier ensemble members that show \nportions of northeast Iowa and southwest Wisconsin receiving 3 to 6\" \nof snow, however the probabilities for this to occur are less than \n10%. Stay tuned for future forecast updates. \n\nThe Weekend:  Mild and Mostly Dry\n\nAs the upper level low shifts off to the northeast, the cooler air \ngoes with it for the end of the week. Weak zonal flow takes over the \nupper air pattern for Friday and Saturday resulting in some warmer \nweather to end the week. Highs look to get back into low to mid 40s \nwith some low 50s possible for Saturday. Cooler air returns this \nSunday as a weak cold front moves through the region. This front \nalso brings about precipitation chances, however they are only \nbetween 10 and 15%. The better precipitation chances occur east of \nthe local region as the trough is expected to deepen after it \npasses our CWA.",
